{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1453134-gangrel-survival-tactics-in-rural-areas","id":1453134,"title":"Gangrel survival tactics in rural areas","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-06-22T03:33:11-07:00","id":1453134,"title":"Gangrel survival tactics in rural areas","content":"How do Gangrel, especially young Gangrel who can't yet turn into bats, survive in rural areas for years, even decades at a time? Is it just because werewolf numbers and vigilance are overrated? Do they stick strictly to roads and truckstops, never actually venturing into the forests? That is where the hitchhikers and truckdrivers they presumably eat are, but seems rather dull for creatures who enjoy the wilderness. Are Gangrel effectively banned from the forests they love until they can fly and view werewolf territory from on high?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T04:08:45-07:00","id":1453135,"title":"","content":"I would go with:<br /> <br /> - areas with werewolfs are rare<br /> - better know you are not the largest fish in the pond and run<br /> - many do not make it/ you meet the lucky ones<br /> - nature skills (see that flower yellow XX marks so and so territory)<br /> - powers (sense the beast, auras) to detect and avoid dangerous areas<br /> <br /> Gangel in particular might have traditions (always stay west of the river XX, cover yourself in that sort of earth) or some sort of pact (spirit X if appeased will warn them to leave, talk to the bats).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26030,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26030-mfalkenb","user":"mfalkenb","date":". 06-22-2021, 07:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26030-mfalkenb","name":"mfalkenb","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"49"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T05:24:12-07:00","id":1453140,"title":"","content":"From a rules perspective, Wwolves are obviously overpowered in 1+ on 1 combat situations, but in regards to a conduct of common sense I'd say they're both good to avoid each other. Gangrel are not the enemy per se, they just smell funny. They actually have more enemies in common than compete for similar resources. But once you start exchanging aggravated damage (lesson learned: beware of bat bites!), a quiet and peaceful Masquerade turns into Apocalypse (&quot;disclosure&quot; ... btw is this an intended opposition like &quot;Ascension&quot; and &quot;Descent&quot;?) and a formerly neutral element suddenly becomes a well-connected enemy whose human pawns start turning the tribal environment into something that heavily favors anaerobic specimen. It's like blood does indeed run deep, Shadowlord Elders may remember stories of how these things tend to go south quickly. And after all, there is not much rural area left worldwide anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26600-nehekhare","name":"Nehekhare","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T09:24:25-07:00","id":1453174,"title":"","content":"not all rural areas are lupine infested to the point it's impenetrabel by gangrel, it's not unreasonable to assume that a gangrel can just avoid them even inexperienced gangrel could avoid them even if unintended.i would say animalism is actually more usefull for avoiding lupines then protean is. other then that there's still a few tricks such as recognizing territory markers, having local gangrel warn about lupine locations, using water ways (lupines still need to breath as far as i know) etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25992-archderd","name":"archderd","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25992_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T12:01:08-07:00","id":1453191,"title":"","content":"I find the insistence on nigh-total intersplat ignorance to be monotonous and rather unrealistic if these creatures are meant to have been bumping elbows for literal centuries. Experienced/properly mentored Gangrel should probably know to look for certain signs suggestive of entering the territory of a Garou pack or proximity to a cairn---ritual mounds, glyphs slashed on rocks and trees, etc. If they see the signs suggesting Here Be Lupines, they simply give it a wide berth. There are not nearly enough Werwolves in the world to protect every acre of wilderness, and even the theoretical territories of large Septs must include large swathes unmonitored from time to time.<br /> <br /> Nor do I think it especially dangerous for the Outlanders to maintain a subsistence and the Masquerade in a rural area, although it is inevitably tedious and lonely compared to the unlife of the Gilded Cage. Barns and sheds, esp. those left derelict, can make decent resstops with a ghouled hellhound or other <i>famulus</i> to ward people away by day. Farmhouses in the Americas are generally well isolated and provide a night's easy feeding, whereas in the Old World, country villages are likely to have long-established &quot;understandings&quot; with local nightgaunts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26710,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26710-ventrue-busboy","user":"Ventrue Busboy","date":". 06-22-2021, 03:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26710-ventrue-busboy","name":"Ventrue Busboy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26710_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T17:40:48-07:00","id":1453217,"title":"","content":"Garou tend ( lets leave all the urban and suburban Bawns aside for the time being) to be concentrated around areas of primary forest or places that humans have barely disturbed, that leaves a lot of regrowth and plantation areas, ranches, scrubland, old industrial areas that are being reclaimed, strips of woodlands that are too small to support kinfolk populations and a bunch of other places.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T18:39:40-07:00","id":1453222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Damian May</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1453134-gangrel-survival-tactics-in-rural-areas?p=1453217#post1453217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Garou tend ( lets leave all the urban and suburban Bawns aside for the time being) to be concentrated around areas of primary forest or places that humans have barely disturbed, that leaves a lot of regrowth and plantation areas, ranches, scrubland, old industrial areas that are being reclaimed, strips of woodlands that are too small to support kinfolk populations and a bunch of other places.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually an interesting idea. Werewolves would presumably fight very hard to, for example, keep an old growth hardwood forest pristine. When they fail, and a forest gets cut down and replaced with cheap fast growing pine, they likely often abandon it. Which is a great time for the Gangrel to move in. Gangrel don't care that the area has been spiritually destroyed by destroying thousand year old hardwood trees. The new pine forest has animals to eat and play with, and is largely inaccessible and undesirable to vampires without Protean.<br /> <br /> In fact, this might be the sort of thing the Gangrel bargain for in Primogen meetings. An ancient forest full of sacred sites is useless to the Gangrel, but it's equally useless if the area gets completely developed and urbanized. But an ancient forest that gets chopped down and desecrated and then replaced with pines that will be chopped down in turn periodically is very useful to them.<br /> <br /> That might be worth, say, the Gangrel Primogen ordering his/her progeny to lend military aid to that Ventrue who has no desire to risk himself or his progeny fighting Sabbat or Anarchs. Cancel your plans to expand the city into that forest, but make sure it gets chopped down and replanted, and I'll put three Gangrel ancillae on the case of killing your enemies for you.<br /> <br /> Ranches are useful, too. Plenty of cows and cowboys to eat. Plenty of open ground to earthmeld into.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3598-cajunkhan","name":"CajunKhan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3598_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1700"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T18:56:06-07:00","id":1453224,"title":"","content":"I think somewhere the books said that there are approximately 300,000 humans per werewolf, as opposed to 100,000 per vampire. It\u2019s harder to compare because werewolves would be most common where humans are the least numerous, but let\u2019s go with that ratio for now.<br /> <br /> That means that in the USA and Canada that there would be about 1,200 werewolves. To put that number into perspective, there are about 50 to 60 thousand bears in the USA and Canada. How many of us have even seen a bear in the wild? I have, but I am an avid hiker in a mountainous area of the USA with state and national parks. I bet that very few Americans have seen a wild bear.<br /> <br /> There are 50 times as many bears as werewolves and huge swaths of wilderness. Werewolves also operate in packs and will be concentrated around areas of mystical power, like Cairns, not evenly distributed, making it even less likely to encounter them at random.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, werewolves are busy fighting creatures of the Wyrm that are actively destroying Gaia or otherwise causing corruption. They aren\u2019t just patrolling the country side in search of any Wyrm creature that they can find, especially one that seemingly has very little environmental impact, like a Gangrel. <br /> <br /> If a Gangrel doesn\u2019t go into Garou territory, then the chance of running across them at random is very low.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-22T19:14:11-07:00","id":1453225,"title":"","content":"There's a variety of answers depending on the specifics about Lupines you decide on for your chronicles. The answer in everyone's chronicles will be different based on what specifics they choose for their chronicle. I think there are several things to keep in mind.<br /> <br /> 1) While there are areas of extreme danger for any vampire (likely any place near a kinfolk community, a caern, or a particular protectorate defended by a specific pack), these are not omnipresent in the setting. An ST needs to make decisions about what areas of definitely off limits, what areas represent a heightened danger, what areas are of routine danger (for non-urban), and which areas are probably just as safe as the large urban metros. The Gangrel clan probably has significant knowledge of the boundaries of these areas (simply by the long experience of their members who know when Gangrel had disappeared and are assumed dead) and shares them informally with new members. These boundaries likely adjust over time, but slow enough that the bulk of Gangrel are protected at any one time until they figure out the new map. The VtM's game claims of the outlands being extremely dangerous because of Lupines has some basis in fact, but is probably more due to great uncertainty and a lack of knowledge, not that it is actually instant death every time you leave the city.<br /> <br /> 2) The Gangrel advantage of not detecting as Wyrm with lower levels of Humanity is probably a big advantage as a lazy way to search for vampires is probably just by using that Gift. This likely helps fledgling Gangrel a lot since most newly made vampires are likely to be of higher Humanity anyway and be extremely cautious when it comes to Masquerade (and it's not like most vampires have to be taught the importance of the concept even if they don't yet know the Traditions).<br /> <br /> 3) Gangrel being constantly on the move works to their advantage. Lupines are territorial creatures and likely prone not to keep pursuing a lead once a possible or detected threat seems to have left. A Gangrel doing something suspicious in one small town is likely gone the next day or by the next week. There's likely not a lot of time for even local Lupines to become aware there is a vampire in the area before they are gone. Most aren't looking to lay down roots, build influence, or have a permanent haven. In contrast, your Ventrue or Toreador will do just that, and a long enough time gets them discovered and killed.<br /> <br /> 4) While Lupines in general hate vampires, there have always been significant exceptions as certain tribes, camps, factions, or individuals, have some kind of dealings with certain individuals. It's a business arrangement, not friendship, and such deals have to be kept on the download. But there's probably enough such Lupine &quot;friends&quot; to give enough hints and warnings to individuals that keeps them away from the greatest areas of danger as they travel. (In exchange, those vampires do something in exchange for their &quot;friends.&quot;) Newly made Gangrel can't take advantage of this, but long lived Gangrel could very well have long standing associations with certain Lupines that have been made over their long lives and even passed down to childer or coterie mates. And some of this knowledge will spread throughout the clan per point 1 above.<br /> <br /> 5) The actual wilderness is very big and very isolated. This link has a map that shows <a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/nik-freeman-map-of-us-population-2014-4\" target=\"_blank\">the 47% of the US that has no one living in that area at all</a>. And lots of area outside that has <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/US_population_map.png\" target=\"_blank\">extremely low population density</a>. Now while these are the areas the Garou are most likely to be, they're still going to be concentrated in only certain areas. A lot of wilderness isn't near a caern or kinfolk settlement. As long as the Gangrel is willing to feed on animals, they could likely survive for a long time. How many caerns does even a state like Wyoming have? Much of the state is probably &quot;open&quot; in regards to the Gangrel who have the powers best suited to survive there and are less likely to be randomly detected as Wyrm. If they act smart, they should be OK even though Wyoming is likely a stronghold of the Lupines relatively. The continental US and Europe is not like Canada or Alaska that still has a lot of wolves, and therefore a greater number of Lupines based on that population instead of the human population. In fact, simply avoiding areas that are known to have wolves probably eliminates a lot of danger for Gangrel combined with some other knowledge.<br /> <br /> 6) Certain lineages of Gangrel likely have other allies that will protect them. For example, in the first Sabbat books, they made a big deal that many nomadic &quot;Country Gangrel&quot; were of American Indian stock (likely because the setting history says it was the Sabbat who first came to the New World, and this is pre-revised Code of Milan and Purchase Pact that saw the Sabbat change). I always imagined that most of these Indian Gangrel were the old followers of the Path of Harmony. So in some of these Indian communities, there may be a heritage of &quot;strange people&quot; or &quot;spirits&quot; (or whatever euphemism is used for these Gangrel) visiting them so they have safe spots they can always come back to as they travel back and forth.<br /> <br /> Newly embraced, inexperienced Gangrel likely survive through a combination of luck, and their sires not dumping them off in an area known to be hostile until the time comes when they find them again and teach them the official ways. Then they pick up enough knowledge that probably keeps them safe a significant amount of time.<br /> <br /> The other clans lack much of the informal knowledge the Gangrel keep so they frequently stumble into situations that kill them because they have no idea that small town inbetween Cleveland and Cincinnati is known to be deadly (because it's a kinfolk community that is well guarded by Lupines), but it's such a convenient spot for a vampire to &quot;lay low&quot; when fleeing one city for the next, that they keep getting killed. So the Kindred of Ohio think the state is a death trap. But the Gangrel just know - stay away from that county. It's dangerous. Go off on the sideroads to the east, and you'll be fine as long as you don't start trouble or act stupid.<br /> <br /> \u200bThere's probably some other things you can stick in there, but it's probably a good set of principles for an ST to begin with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T01:24:08-07:00","id":1453254,"title":"","content":"Honestly most of the rural areas should be pretty safe becuase the wilderness is humungous and werewolves are rare<br /> <br /> <b>When a vampire dies in the wilderness is becuase</b><ul><li>It hits humanity zero becuase of lack of human contact.</li> <li>It frenzies due to not be able to properly hunt (ex ventrue or old enough vampire to be unable to feed of animal blood) so werewolves or camarilla agents kill him to preserve the masquerade.</li> <li>It stays too long in an area and eventually a roamming werewolf pack catches him alone</li> <li>Rarely they came across werewolf territory wich is a big fuck up</li> <li>Without protean finding a haven in the wild is quite difficult</li> <li>Werewolves fight the vampire who is alone and stops in small towns to refuel , they just stalk him until morning</li> </ul><br /> <br /> Note:In my games werewolves can comitt diablerie to increase their equivalent to blood potency so...they actually patrol the borderlands to see if they can catch unaware vampires who are stupid enough to leave the protection of the group AKA the city where they are outnumbered.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.deviantart.com/cicerondixit/gallery</a>","edited":{"userid":24037,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","user":"Leandro16","date":". 06-23-2021, 04:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24037-leandro16","name":"Leandro16","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24037_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"552"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T06:09:17-07:00","id":1453268,"title":"","content":"In most modern nights games, I play lupines as more a memetic threat and convenient bogeyman used by most to explain the unexplained -- or as a cover story for goings-on a city's elders would prefer <i>not</i> be known. Vampire traveling between cities gets merced by...<br /> <br /> ...a group of hunters? Lupines did it!<br /> ...a rival or enemy? Lupines did it!<br /> ...the sherrif? Lupines did it!<br /> ...Anarchs or neonates/ancillae out for a diablerie? Lupines did it!<br /> ...a Sabbat pack? Lupines did it!<br /> ...their own inability to feed, or find shelter from daylight outside the city? Lupines did it!<br /> <br /> Or maybe a vampire just ran off to...<br /> <br /> ...defect from their clan? Lupines did it!<br /> ...defect to another sect? Lupines did it!<br /> ...avoid a blood hunt? Lupines did it!<br /> ...avoid a vengeful elder or sire? Lupines did it!<br /> ...hide they learned too much? Lupines did it!<br /> <br /> The latter category being a common cause for the former, of course.<br /> <br /> For my games it's the quintessential &quot;dog ate my homework&quot; of the vampire world, the subtext for those who've been around a few decades being a wink-and-nod &quot;it's best for everyone if nobody investigates this, if you know what I'm saying&quot;. It's not exactly falsifiable under most circumstances, and lupines are enough of a meme to make sure only the stupid and ambitious (read, those best eliminated from the &quot;gene pool&quot; at earliest opportunity) go sniffing around where they shouldn't. But at the same time, practically every Kindred has &quot;a buddy of a buddy&quot; who tangled with a lupine once...or at least thought they did...and miraculously lived to tell the tale lupines are not to be fucked with.<br /> <br /> Not that elders with Dominate, Protean, Obfuscate, Vicissitude and a creative bent, Chimerstry, or in a pinch well-trained ghouls in convincing costumes, would have a vested interest in faking a lupine attack every once in a while, and letting their victims escape to perpetuate the tale.<br /> <br /> As far as <i>actual</i> dealings with lupines...I tend to play it by ear, depending on what clans and character archetypes are being played, and what themes I'm prioritizing in a given game. I've played them as monster movie lupines, lycanthropy and all, at times, and others I've gone so far as to name them by tribe although I stay dodgy on <i>what</i> it is they do or why they exist. The one thing I <i>never</i> do is cross over WtA crunch with VtM crunch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T08:37:44-07:00","id":1453287,"title":"","content":"Even ignoring the absurd size of the USA and instead focusing on more developed lands like western europe, there's still just tons of unused land. Unless a vampire goes wondering into a caern, a lupine isn't going to discern that a person in the distance is a vampire. Lupines don't spend time fanning out searching for undead that've gone for a midnight stroll, if it's not the Caern then it's a waste of resources. Furthermore, Lupines like pure wilds, while Gangrel are looking for something more rural/the outskirts of town/a village. <br /> <br /> 1- Lupines are mortal, and though they heal at fast rates, that doesn't put oxygen into their muscles. A vampire can presumably continue to sprint all night, so while a Gangrel without 4 Protean or crazy physicals is likely to be run down within a hundred metres, the gangrel will win any long distance chase. <br /> 2- Fortitude would help the vampire run through spiked bushes without getting caught by the thorns. <br /> 3- Bodies of water provide a great escape option for the vampire, who doesn't need to worry about drowning or cold. Shallow water may give lupines an advantage however, as crinos gives them an easier time wading through the water. <br /> 4- silver is relatively cheap and you can carry a lot of it on you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-23T10:30:27-07:00","id":1453296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CajunKhan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1453134-gangrel-survival-tactics-in-rural-areas#post1453134\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How do Gangrel, especially young Gangrel who can't yet turn into bats, survive in rural areas for years, even decades at a time? Is it just because werewolf numbers and vigilance are overrated? Do they stick strictly to roads and truckstops, never actually venturing into the forests? That is where the hitchhikers and truckdrivers they presumably eat are, but seems rather dull for creatures who enjoy the wilderness. Are Gangrel effectively banned from the forests they love until they can fly and view werewolf territory from on high?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Depends on where on Earth they prowl, I suspect. Here in France, it's relatively easy to move from hamlet to hamlet over all the territory. If they are willing to roam around the edges of larger conurbations, it's even easier. Plus, cattle and the like can offer a relatively accessible and easy way to get some amount of Vitae for young Gangrels. They also probably learn real fast to read the warning signs of the Lupines - those who don't die even faster.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1233-ur-than","name":"Ur-Than","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1233_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"253"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-25T18:08:20-07:00","id":1453497,"title":"","content":"To add to everyone else's points, I contend that the &quot;meme&quot; of Lupines hiding just beyond the city limits, waiting to slaughter any vampire that ventures out, is a bit of &quot;Old Vampire Wisdom&quot;.<br /> <br /> Which is to say, it's how Elders interpreted the world back when they unlived during pre-modern times, and that they continue to teach their Childer, regardless of how applicable it is. Same with a lot of what baby boomers tell their kids, about employment or the affordability of housing. They cannot internalize that conditions would have changed so drastically from their (un)lived experiences.<br /> <br /> In medieval times, the number of mortals and &quot;developed&quot; land was far fewer, and the wilderness and Lupines much more abundant. It made sense that if a vampire wanders too far into the forest, they'd get ganked by a random encounter of wolf men. Nor did a medieval Kindred have much recourse when it came to crossing those gulfs of wilderness quickly or easily.<br /> <br /> Nowadays, there's way fewer Lupines, way more &quot;developed countryside&quot; (farms, ranches, parks, etc.), and far more expedient ways of getting from city to city. Heck, driving a car alleviates a vast majority of dangers to a vampire on the road, because they can cross hundreds of miles in a single night AND use the trunk of their car as impromptu shelter if needed. At least when it comes to Lupines, a car moves too fast to even be noticed.<br /> <br /> But the meme persists, because that's just what every Kindred has been told by their sires and reinforced through peer consensus.<br /> <br /> Heck, Gangrel likely work to <i>perpetuate</i> this perception. Insisting that the land outside the cities is still dangerous AF and full of furry terrors. They do this for two reasons. One, to discourage other vampires from building influence in more rural areas, allowing the Gangrel to keep it all for themselves. And two, so when a Gangrel is being hired to act as guide or courier or smuggler or whatever, they can extract greater Boons, by leaning on the idea that the non-urban areas are a death trap and only they, the Gangrel, know how to navigate it. It makes Gangrel indispensable to ignorant and paranoid Kindred.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}